# CHAPTER 3: "ADJUSTMENT"

---

Five days.

That's how long Kai spent in Stitch's clinic before he could walk without the world tilting sideways. Five days of his body learning to trust the thing in his spine, the foreign presence that shouldn't exist but did.

Five days of pretending everything was fine when Mira visited.

Stitch kept her out of the main clinic during those first days—said Kai needed rest, needed quiet, needed time. She believed him. Brought drawings, taped them to the wall where Kai could see them from the operating table turned recovery bed. Stick figures labeled "FAMILY." Always holding hands.

Kai stared at them when the pain got bad. When his body rejected the Surge's integration and Stitch had to administer more immunosuppressants. When phantom sensations crawled up his spine like insects made of electricity.

The drawings reminded him why he was still breathing.

Why he'd accepted this thing inside him.

---

Day six, Stitch sat him down.

The clinic was quiet. Mira was at the street food stall with Dax—Stitch had asked him to keep her occupied for a few hours. Gave them creds for food, told Mira to get whatever she wanted.

She'd left happy. Didn't know this was the conversation Kai had been dreading.

Stitch pulled up a chair. Medical datapad in hand, but he didn't look at it. Just looked at Kai. The way a father looks at a son before delivering bad news.

"We need to talk about what's in you."

Kai's jaw tightened. "The Surge."

"Synaptic Combat Override. Mark VII. Atlas Dynamics military prototype." Stitch set the datapad aside. "I need you to understand what it is. What it does. What it costs."

"It saved my life."

"It did. Your spinal cord was severed at T11. You were paralyzed, bleeding internally, minutes from death. The Surge bypassed the damage—connected your brain stem directly to your nervous system. You're walking because it's bridging the gap."

Kai knew this. Had felt it. The wrongness of moving legs that shouldn't work.

"But there's more," Stitch continued. "The Surge isn't just a medical implant. It's a combat system. Designed to turn soldiers into weapons. And it comes with... features."

"The Voice."

Stitch nodded. "When you woke up, you heard it. Initialization protocol—the system establishing connection, running diagnostics. You thought it would talk constantly."

"It doesn't?"

"No. The Voice only activates during combat situations. High stress, elevated heart rate, adrenaline spike—the Surge interprets these as threat conditions and engages tactical AI support."

Kai's hands clenched. "An AI. Giving me orders."

"Not orders. Suggestions. Threat assessment, tactical recommendations, optimal responses. It's designed to enhance battlefield performance—help soldiers make better decisions under pressure." Stitch's expression darkened. "But it's still learning. Every fight teaches it more about you. Your patterns, your instincts, your weaknesses."

"And I can't remove it."

"If I remove it, your spine is still severed. You'll be paralyzed. Or dead." Stitch leaned forward. "But you CAN control it. The Voice offers advice—you don't have to follow it. You maintain command of your own body. Your own choices."

"Unless?"

Stitch hesitated. "Unless you're under extreme stress. Trauma triggers, overwhelming danger, situations that pattern-match to... previous experiences. Then the Surge can override your consciousness. Take full control."

The words sat heavy between them.

"It turns me into a weapon."

"Yes."

"How often?"

"I don't know. The Mark VII was experimental. Atlas shut down the program because prototypes showed... instability. Operators losing control, hurting civilians, unable to deactivate." Stitch's voice was quiet. "I'm sorry, Kai. I saved your life, but I don't know if I did you a favor."

Kai looked at his hands. They were steady. His. For now.

"Can you teach me? To control it?"

"I can teach you suppression techniques. Mental discipline, neural feedback training. It won't remove the Voice, but it'll help you keep it quiet. In normal combat, you'll have control. You can choose to listen or ignore it."

"And when it takes over completely?"

Stitch's expression was stone. "Then you survive. And you pray you don't remember what you did."

---

The training started that afternoon.

Stitch's clinic had a back room—originally storage, now converted into something between a gym and a meditation chamber. Mats on the floor, medical monitoring equipment along one wall, a heavy bag hanging in the corner.

Kai sat cross-legged on a mat. Stitch across from him.

"The Surge feeds on adrenaline," Stitch explained. "Stress, fear, anger—these trigger its activation. So we start with the opposite. Calm. Control. Breathing."

"I'm not meditating my way out of this."

"You're learning to recognize when it's trying to activate. Before it speaks, there's a shift—neurological, chemical. You'll feel it if you pay attention. A pressure at the base of your skull, a spike in awareness, time seeming to slow slightly."

Kai closed his eyes. Focused on breathing. Felt nothing except frustration.

"It won't work if you're fighting it," Stitch said. "The Surge is part of you now. Acknowledge that. Accept it. Then learn to coexist."

"I don't want to coexist with a weapon in my brain."

"You don't have a choice. So you can either let it control you, or you learn to manage it." Stitch's voice was firm. Not unkind. Just truth. "Breathe. Focus. Find the boundary between you and it."

Kai tried. Minutes passed. Nothing.

Then—there. Faint. A presence at the edge of his awareness. Not thinking, not speaking. Just... there. Watching. Waiting.

His eyes snapped open. "I felt it."

"Good. That's the first step. Recognizing it exists." Stitch handed him a small bottle. Pills. "Neural stabilizers. Take one every morning. They reduce the activation threshold—make the Voice quieter, harder to trigger."

Kai looked at the bottle. Another dependency. Another chain.

"Will Mira ask about these?"

"Tell her they're vitamins. Recovery supplements." Stitch's expression softened. "She doesn't need to know, Kai. Not yet. Maybe not ever."

Kai pocketed the pills. Another lie to add to the weight.

---

They trained for three days.

Breathing exercises. Neural feedback—Stitch hooked Kai up to monitors, taught him to watch his own brainwave patterns, identify when the Surge was trying to engage. Mental discipline—recognizing intrusive thoughts, distinguishing his voice from the Voice.

Sparring. Light at first, then harder. Stitch wore padded gloves, threw controlled strikes. Kai defended, countered, stayed present.

Waiting for the Voice.

It didn't come. Not during training. Stress wasn't high enough, threat wasn't real enough.

"It knows," Kai muttered after the third session. "It knows this isn't real combat."

"Good," Stitch said. "Means it's sophisticated. Means you'll have warning before it activates—real danger, not sparring."

Kai wasn't sure if that was comforting or terrifying.

---

Mira noticed the changes.

"You're different," she said one evening. They were eating dinner—Stitch's cooking, simple but warm. "You're quieter."

Kai forced a smile. "Just tired. Still recovering."

"From what? You said it was a bad job, but you didn't say what happened." Her eyes were too knowing. Too perceptive. "Did someone hurt you?"

"Yeah. But I'm okay now."

"You're taking pills. I saw them."

Stitch intervened smoothly. "Recovery supplements. Helps his body heal faster. Nothing to worry about."

Mira frowned but accepted it. Went back to her food.

Kai met Stitch's eyes across the table. Silent agreement: She can never know.

Later, after Mira went to bed, Kai pulled out his journal. First entry since the incident.

*Day nine post-Surge installation. Training going well. Stitch says I'm learning fast. Haven't heard the Voice yet outside of waking up. Don't know if that's good or bad.*

*Mira asked questions today. Had to lie. Again. I hate it. But what's the alternative? Tell her I have military AI in my brain that might turn me into a killer? She's fourteen. She's been through enough.*

*Stitch says the Voice only activates in real combat. Tomorrow I'm taking a job. Small. Low-risk. Debt collection for a local merchant. Just to test.*

*I'm afraid of what I'll hear.*

He closed the journal. Stared at the pendant around his neck—brother's pendant, all he had left.

Wondered if his brother would recognize him now.

Wondered if he'd recognize himself.

---

The job was simple.

Merchant in Driftmarket—owed money by a small-time thug who'd been dodging payment for weeks. Nothing violent. Just "apply pressure," as the merchant put it. Make the guy understand consequences.

Kai took it. Needed the creds. Needed to test the Surge in controlled conditions.

Needed to know if he was still in control.

Driftmarket at night was alive with neon and desperation. Black market vendors hawking stolen tech, augment clinics advertising discount installations, street food stalls grilling synthetic meat that smelled better than it tasted. Crowds moved like schools of fish—fast, alert, ready to scatter at the first sign of VSD.

Kai's Vantage Optics tracked automatically. Exits, threats, faces. Old habits. Atlas training layered over present awareness.

He found the thug in an alley behind a chop shop. Mid-twenties, cheap chrome, holding a bottle of something that probably wasn't legal. Two friends with him—backup or just company.

Kai approached. Kept his hands visible. Non-threatening. Professional.

"You're Voss?"

The thug looked up. Bloodshot eyes, augmented but low-grade. "Who's asking?"

"Merchant on Fifth Street says you owe him fifteen hundred creds. I'm here to collect."

Voss laughed. His friends joined in. "You're the collection agency? One guy?"

"One's enough."

"I don't think so." Voss set the bottle down. Stood. His friends flanked him. "Tell your merchant I'll pay when I feel like it. Now fuck off before—"

He moved.

Fast. Faster than Kai expected. Knife from his jacket, slashing toward Kai's throat.

Kai's training kicked in—

Then the Voice spoke.

Not loud. Not commanding. Just... there. In his head. Calm. Clinical.

**"HOSTILE DETECTED. THREAT LEVEL: MINIMAL. WEAPON: BLADE. RECOMMENDED ACTION: DISARM. SECONDARY OPTION: LETHAL FORCE."**

Time didn't slow. Didn't speed up. Just sharpened. Every detail crystalline.

Kai's body moved—sidestepped the knife, grabbed Voss's wrist, twisted. The blade clattered to the pavement.

**"DISARM SUCCESSFUL. THREAT REDUCED. ADDITIONAL HOSTILES: TWO. ASSESSING."**

Voss's friends were moving—one reaching for a gun, the other circling left.

**"OPTIMAL RESPONSE: NEUTRALIZE ARMED HOSTILE FIRST. PRIORITY TARGET: RIGHT FLANK."**

Kai's fist was already moving. Caught the gun-holder in the solar plexus. The man folded. Gun dropped.

**"SECONDARY THREAT APPROACHING. RECOMMEND: DISABLE, NON-LETHAL."**

The third thug swung—amateur, telegraphed. Kai ducked, swept his legs. The man hit concrete hard.

**"THREATS NEUTRALIZED. MISSION: SUCCESSFUL. RECOMMEND SECURING PRIMARY TARGET."**

Voss was on the ground, clutching his wrist. Kai stood over him. Not winded. Not even breathing hard.

The Voice was still there. Waiting. Analyzing.

Kai looked at Voss. Saw fear. Saw a punk in over his head.

**"PRIMARY TARGET SUBDUED. RECOMMENDED NEXT ACTION: EXTRACT PAYMENT OR TERMINATE CONTRACT."**

"Shut up," Kai whispered.

**"QUERY: NEGATIVE RESPONSE UNCLEAR. CLARIFY OPERATOR INTENT."**

"I said shut up." Louder now.

Voss looked confused. "What?"

Kai blinked. Realized he'd spoken aloud. "Not you."

He pulled Voss up by the collar. "Fifteen hundred creds. Now."

Voss fumbled with his Data Tap. Transferred payment—Kai's Vantage Optics confirmed receipt.

"We're done. Stay done."

He let go. Voss scrambled back. His friends picked themselves up. All three ran.

Kai stood alone in the alley.

The Voice was quiet. Not gone. Just... waiting.

**"COMBAT ENGAGEMENT: COMPLETE. OPERATOR PERFORMANCE: ADEQUATE. RECOMMENDATION: INCREASE AGGRESSION THRESHOLD FOR OPTIMAL EFFICIENCY."**

"No."

**"QUERY: NEGATIVE RESPONSE TO EFFICIENCY OPTIMIZATION?"**

"I'm not optimizing. I'm surviving."

**"NOTED. ADAPTATION LOGGED."**

Then silence.

Kai's hands shook. Not from fear. From adrenaline crash. From the realization of what just happened.

The Voice had helped. Had given tactical advice. And he'd followed it.

It had worked.

But it hadn't been him. Not entirely.

He looked at his hands. They'd moved with precision he didn't remember choosing. Muscle memory layered with something else. Something calculated.

Stitch's words echoed: *You'll have control. You can choose to listen or ignore it.*

But Kai hadn't ignored it. He'd listened. And his body had obeyed before his mind caught up.

That was the trap. Not losing control. But accepting the help. Letting it guide him.

Becoming dependent.

---

He returned to the clinic. Stitch was waiting.

"How'd it go?"

Kai sat heavily. "The Voice activated. Gave tactical suggestions. I followed them."

"Did you have control?"

"I think so. It offered advice. I could've ignored it." He paused. "I didn't."

"Why not?"

"Because it was right. It saw the threats before I processed them consciously. Recommended responses that worked. I fought better with it than I would've without it."

Stitch's expression was unreadable. "And that scares you."

"Yeah."

"Good. Fear means you're paying attention." Stitch poured tea. Old ritual. Calming. "The Surge is a tool, Kai. Like your Vantage Optics, your pistol, your training. It enhances what you already know. The danger isn't using it. The danger is forgetting you're the one in charge."

"But I listened to it."

"You chose to listen. There's a difference." Stitch handed him a cup. "You could've ignored it and fought your way. You had that choice. You decided its input was valuable. That's not losing control. That's tactical thinking."

Kai wanted to believe that. Wasn't sure he could.

"What if next time I don't have a choice?"

"Then we deal with it. But tonight, you had control. That's progress." Stitch sipped his tea. "The Voice will get louder. More insistent. It's learning from you, adapting. But as long as you maintain awareness—as long as you question it—you're still you."

"And when I stop questioning?"

"Then we have a problem."

---

Kai wrote in his journal that night.

*First combat with the Surge. The Voice activated immediately. Calm. Clinical. Tactical.*

*It told me where threats were, what to do, how to respond. I followed its advice. Fight ended in seconds. No injuries. Clean.*

*But I can't stop thinking: How much of that was me? How much was it?*

*My hands moved before I decided to move them. Like the training was automated. Optimized. Efficient.*

*Stitch says I had control. Says I chose to listen.*

*I want to believe that.*

*But I'm afraid of the day I stop choosing.*

He closed the journal. Touched the pendant.

Thought of his brother. Wondered if he'd fought this same battle. If Atlas had put something in him too.

If they'd both been turned into weapons without realizing it until too late.

Mira knocked softly. "Kai? You awake?"

He shoved the journal into his jacket. "Yeah, kid. Come in."

She entered. Pajamas, hair loose, clutching a drawing. "I made this. For you."

She handed it over. Crayon on paper. Three figures: Kai, Mira, Stitch. Labeled "FAMILY" in careful letters. All holding hands.

Same as always. But this time, Kai's figure had a smile.

"You've been sad," Mira said quietly. "I wanted to remind you we're happy you're here."

His throat tightened. "I know, kid."

"Are you okay? Really?"

He looked at her. Fourteen years old. Too perceptive. Too kind. Everything good in a world trying to crush goodness.

"I'm getting there."

She hugged him. He hugged back.

Behind her, through the doorway, Stitch watched. Silent approval. Shared weight.

Mira pulled back. "Good night, Kai."

"Good night."

She left. Door closed softly.

Kai stared at the drawing. Taped it to the wall next to the others.

A family of stick figures. Holding hands. Smiling.

He touched the pendant under his shirt. Brother's pendant. The past he couldn't save.

Looked at the drawing. The present he couldn't lose.

And felt the Surge humming in his spine. The future he couldn't escape.

Somewhere between the three, Kai existed. Trying to hold on.

Trying not to let go.
